    {
      "id": "get-max-tree-depth",
      "name": "GetMaxTreeDepth",
      "category": "SparseDynamicOctree3",
      "signature": "GetMaxTreeDepth(Target)",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "Target",
          "type": "FSparseDynamicOctree3*"
        }
      ],
      "returnType": "Int",
      "docs": "FSparseDynamicOctree3 sorts objects with axis-aligned bounding boxes into a dynamic\nsparse octree of axis-aligned uniform grid cells. At the top level we have an infinite\ngrid of \"root cells\" of size RootDimension, which then contain 8 children, and so on.\n(So in fact each cell is a separate octree, and we have a uniform grid of octrees)\nThe objects and their bounding-boxes are not stored in the tree. You must have an\ninteger identifier (ObjectID) for each object, and call Insert(ObjectID, BoundingBox).\nSome query functions will require you to provide a lambda/etc that can be called to\nretrieve the bounding box for a given ObjectID.\nObjects are currently inserted at the maximum possible depth, ie smallest cell that\nwill contain them, or MaxTreeDepth. The tree boxes are expanded by MaxExpandFactor\nto allow for deeper insertion. If MaxExpandFactor > 0 then the tree does not strictly\npartition space, IE adjacent cells overlap.\nThe octree is dynamic. Objects can be removed and re-inserted."
    },
    {
      "id": "set-max-tree-depth",
      "name": "SetMaxTreeDepth",
      "category": "SparseDynamicOctree3",
      "signature": "SetMaxTreeDepth(Target, Max Tree Depth In)",
